    "The Orient Star was a velvet coffin gliding through the inky Polish countryside. Rain lashed against the panoramic windows, blurring the distant farms into ghostly streaks of light, and the rhythmic clatter of the wheels was a hypnotic pulse against the low hum of conversation and the clinking of crystal in the dining car. But beneath the veneer of luxury and the soporific rocking, a current of unease had begun to flow. It started with the missing emerald necklace – a bauble worth more than most passengers’ estates – and culminated with the discovery of Monsieur Dubois, the renowned art collector, slumped in his compartment, a single crimson stain blooming on the pristine white linen of his travelling suit. The train, already isolated by the storm, was now a sealed chamber of secrets, and every face, illuminated by the flickering gaslight, seemed to hold a carefully constructed lie.",
    "The Orient Star was a velvet coffin gliding through the inky Polish countryside. Rain lashed against the panoramic windows, blurring the skeletal trees into streaks of black, and the rhythmic clatter of the wheels was a hypnotic pulse against the low hum of conversation and the clink of crystal in the dining car. But beneath the veneer of luxurious travel, a chill had settled deeper than the November air. It wasn't the cold that prickled the back of my neck, nor the unsettling darkness pressing against the glass. It was the silence that had fallen around Madame Evangeline Moreau, the renowned psychic, a silence broken only by the single crimson stain blossoming on the ivory silk of her gown, and the fact that her eyes, usually alight with unsettling knowing, stared blankly at the ornate ceiling, seeing nothing at all.",
    "The Orient Star was a velvet coffin gliding through the inky Polish countryside. Rain lashed against the panoramic windows, blurring the distant farms into ghostly streaks of light, and the rhythmic clatter of the wheels was a hypnotic pulse against the low hum of conversation and the clinking of crystal in the dining car. But beneath the veneer of luxurious travel, a chill had settled that had nothing to do with the November air. Old Man Hemlock, a renowned art collector, was dead in his compartment, a single crimson stain blossoming on the pristine white of his cravat, and the locked door suggested a killer amongst the forty-odd souls trapped aboard until morning."
